  "notes_context": "These notes were distilled from earlier work in this repository that passed verification. Follow them unless the code or CHANGELOG.md shows they no longer hold.\n- [at-0001] Round money only with round_money: Always round monetary results with ledgerly.money.round_money (half-up to the cent); never use Python's round() or a bare Decimal.quantize(), which use banker's rounding and produce wrong invoice totals.\n  (learned from task E01 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 1x, 1 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0002] Normalize money via to_money and round_money: Convert any monetary input with `to_money` from `ledgerly.money` and store it rounded with `round_money`; compare against `ZERO` rather than a literal Decimal.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0003] Raise BillingError with stable snake_case codes: Reject invalid billing input by raising `BillingError(code, detail)` from `ledgerly.errors` with codes like `invalid_state`, `invalid_quantity`, `negative_amount`, and include `repr()` of the offending value.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0004] Exclude bool when validating integer fields: When validating integer inputs such as `quantity`, reject `bool` explicitly with `isinstance(x, bool)` before the `int` check, since `True`/`False` are ints in Python.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)",
